questions.prompt:
**Contains Duplicate (Leetcode Hashing Questions slide, "very basic hash table").** Return `true` if some value appears at least twice in `nums`, `false` if all values are distinct. Use a hash-based STL container.

```cpp
bool containsDuplicate(const vector<int>& nums) {
    // write code here
}
```

Also give the time and space complexity of your solution. Justify it the way we do in class: name the cost of every container operation you use (say average vs worst case for the hash-based ones), and combine them with the loop structure.

questions.answer:
```cpp
bool containsDuplicate(const vector<int>& nums) {
    unordered_set<int> seen;
    for (int x : nums) {
        if (!seen.insert(x).second)   // insert returns pair<iterator,bool>; .second == false means x was already there
            return true;
    }
    return false;
}
```

`unordered_set::insert(x)` returns a `pair<iterator,bool>`; the `bool` is `false` when `x` was already in the set, so one call both tests and inserts. (Equivalent: `if (seen.count(x)) return true; seen.insert(x);`, two hash lookups instead of one.)

**Complexity.**
- The loop runs at most n times (n = `nums.size()`).
- Each `insert` on an `unordered_set<int>` is **O(1) average** (compute `hash(x)`, look in one bucket; the occasional rehash is amortized O(1) per insert) and **O(n) worst case** (every key lands in the same bucket). `.second` is O(1).
- n iterations x O(1) = **O(n) average, O(n^2) worst case**. Extra space: **O(n)** (the set can hold all n distinct values).

**Alternatives (and why this one):** sorting first and comparing neighbors is O(n log n) time with O(1) extra space if you may reorder the input; an ordered `set<int>` is O(n log n) worst case (each insert O(log n)) with O(n) space. The hash set trades space for the best average time.

questions.prompt:
**Contains Duplicate II (HW5 Part 3a).** Given `nums` and an integer `k`, return `true` if there are two *distinct indices* `i != j` with `nums[i] == nums[j]` and `|i - j| <= k`; otherwise `false`. Use a hash-based STL container.

```cpp
bool containsNearbyDuplicate(vector<int>& nums, int k) {
    // write code here
}
```

Hint from the HW: read the `unordered_map::operator[]` documentation. Which of its behaviors would be a bug if you used it only to *check* whether a key is present?

Also give the time and space complexity of your solution. Justify it the way we do in class: name the cost of every container operation you use (say average vs worst case for the hash-based ones), and combine them with the loop structure.

questions.answer:
**Idea (hash map tracking the last position):** map each value to the index of its most recent occurrence. When we meet `nums[i]` again, the closest earlier equal value is exactly the stored one; if even that one is more than `k` away, every older occurrence is farther still, so overwriting with the newer index loses nothing.

```cpp
bool containsNearbyDuplicate(vector<int>& nums, int k) {
    unordered_map<int, int> lastPos;                 // value -> index of its most recent occurrence
    for (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {
        auto it = lastPos.find(nums[i]);             // find does NOT create an entry (unlike operator[])
        if (it != lastPos.end() && i - it->second <= k)
            return true;
        lastPos[nums[i]] = i;                        // insert or overwrite with the newer position
    }
    return false;
}
```

**Why not just `seen[nums[i]]` to test membership?** `operator[]` *creates* an element with a default value (0 for an int) when the key is missing. Using it to look something up silently inserts `nums[i] -> 0`, which makes the map grow and, here, would make a later "was it seen?" test wrong (0 looks like a real index). `find` (or `count`) never inserts. The official HW5 solution does `seen.find(...) != seen.end() && (i - seen[nums[i]]) <= k`, which is fine because the `[]` is only evaluated once `find` has proved the key exists; the version above just avoids the second hash lookup.

**Complexity.** n = `nums.size()`. The loop runs n times; per iteration: `find` O(1) average (O(n) worst), integer arithmetic O(1), `operator[]` assignment O(1) average (O(n) worst; includes amortized rehashing). So **O(n) average, O(n^2) worst case**; space **O(n)** distinct values in the map.

questions.prompt:
**Repeated DNA Sequences (HW5 Part 3b).** A DNA string `s` uses only the letters `A`, `C`, `G`, `T`. Return every 10-letter substring that occurs **more than once** in `s`, each reported once, in any order. Use an `unordered_map` to count how many times each length-10 sequence is seen, and `string::substr(pos, len)` to extract each sequence. A plain `for (size_t i ...)` loop is fine (no iterator needed).

```cpp
vector<string> findRepeatedDnaSequences(const string& s) {
    // write code here
}
```

Hint (HW5): `myMap[x]++` works even when `x` is not yet a key. Why?

Also give the time and space complexity of your solution. Justify it the way we do in class: name the cost of every container operation you use (say average vs worst case for the hash-based ones), and combine them with the loop structure.

questions.answer:
```cpp
vector<string> findRepeatedDnaSequences(const string& s) {
    unordered_map<string, int> count;                // 10-letter sequence -> times seen so far
    vector<string> answer;
    for (size_t i = 0; i + 10 <= s.size(); ++i) {
        string seq = s.substr(i, 10);                // O(10) = O(1)
        if (++count[seq] == 2)                       // operator[] creates 0 on first sight; becomes 2 exactly once
            answer.push_back(seq);                   // report each repeated sequence only once
    }
    return answer;
}
```

**Why `count[seq]` works for counting.** `operator[]` returns a reference to the value for the key, first *creating* the element with a value-initialized `int` (= 0) if the key is absent. So `++count[seq]` turns "absent" into 1 and "present with c" into c+1, in one expression. The pre-increment returns the *new* value, so `== 2` is true exactly when the sequence is seen the second time: it is pushed once, never twice, and no second pass over the map is needed. (The HW5 baseline does `sequence[s.substr(i,10)]++` for all i and then loops over the map pushing every key whose count is > 1; the version above is the "refactored for better runtime" one.)

Loop bound: the last valid start index is `s.size() - 10`, so `i + 10 <= s.size()` (written with `i + 10` on the left so the unsigned `size_t` never underflows when `s` is shorter than 10).

**Complexity** (n = `s.size()`).
- There are n - 9 = O(n) starting positions.
- Per position: `substr(i,10)` copies 10 characters, an "expensive" but constant O(1); hashing a 10-character string is O(10) = O(1); `operator[]` is O(1) average (O(size) worst case); `++`, the comparison and `push_back` (amortized) are O(1).
- Total **O(n) average**, O(n^2) worst case if every key collided. Space: O(n) distinct 10-letter keys (each of constant size).

Code is identical except for the container:

```cpp
vector<string> findRepeatedDnaSequencesOrdered(const string& s) {
    map<string, int> count;                          // identical code, ordered map: every access is O(log n) comparisons
    vector<string> answer;
    for (size_t i = 0; i + 10 <= s.size(); ++i) {
        string seq = s.substr(i, 10);
        if (++count[seq] == 2)
            answer.push_back(seq);
    }
    return answer;
}
```

**Unordered map (hash) version.**
- There are n - 9 = O(n) substrings of length 10.
- Taking a length-10 substring is O(10) = O(1): an "expensive" O(1), but still constant, independent of n.
- Looking the substring up and incrementing it costs one hash computation over 10 characters (another expensive O(1)) plus an access to one bucket: O(1) **average** (O(n) worst case if all keys collide, but a decent hash does not).
- So each substring costs O(1) and there are O(n) of them: **O(n) average**. If we instead make a second pass over the map to collect counts > 1, iterating a hash container is O(n) as long as the table keeps a reasonable load factor, so the total stays **O(n)**.

**Ordered map version.** `map` is a balanced BST (red-black tree in practice). `operator[]` must search the tree: O(log m) *comparisons*, where m <= n - 9 is the number of distinct keys, so O(log n). Each comparison is between two 10-character strings, which is O(10) = O(1). Still, O(n) substrings x O(log n) per access = **O(n log n)** (worst case, deterministic). A pass over the map in order is O(n).

**Comparison:** O(n) average (hash) vs O(n log n) worst case (tree). The tree version is slower asymptotically but has a guaranteed bound and returns the keys in sorted order for free; the hash version is faster on average but its O(n) is an average-case claim.

questions.prompt:
**Contains Duplicate III (Leetcode Hashing Questions slide: "ordered set problem"; HW4 solution).** Given `nums`, `indexDiff` and `valueDiff`, return `true` if there exist indices `i != j` with `|i - j| <= indexDiff` **and** `|nums[i] - nums[j]| <= valueDiff`; otherwise `false`. Assume `indexDiff >= 1`, `valueDiff >= 0`.

Use an ordered `std::set` that always contains only the values in the last `indexDiff` positions (a fixed-size sliding window). Use the *member* function `lower_bound`.

```cpp
bool containsNearbyAlmostDuplicate(vector<int>& nums, int indexDiff, int valueDiff) {
    // write code here
}
```

Then (a) give the time complexity in terms of n = `nums.size()` and k = `indexDiff`, and (b) explain why an `unordered_set` could not replace the `set`.

questions.answer:
```cpp
bool containsNearbyAlmostDuplicate(vector<int>& nums, int indexDiff, int valueDiff) {
    set<long long> window;                           // the values at the last indexDiff positions
    for (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {
        long long x = nums[i];                       // long long so x - valueDiff / x + valueDiff cannot overflow
        auto lb = window.lower_bound(x - valueDiff); // smallest value in the window that is >= x - valueDiff
        if (lb != window.end() && *lb <= x + valueDiff)
            return true;                             // that value lies in [x - valueDiff, x + valueDiff]
        window.insert(x);
        if (i >= indexDiff)                          // keep a fixed-size sliding window
            window.erase((long long)nums[i - indexDiff]);
    }
    return false;
}
```

**How it works.** For each new value x we need *any* earlier value (within the window) in the interval [x - valueDiff, x + valueDiff]. `window.lower_bound(x - valueDiff)` returns the smallest window element that is >= x - valueDiff. If that element exists and is also <= x + valueDiff, it is inside the interval; if it is > x + valueDiff, then every larger element is too, and every smaller one is < x - valueDiff, so no element of the window is in the interval. After the test we insert x and, once i >= indexDiff, erase the value that is now `indexDiff` positions old, so the window always holds exactly the last `indexDiff` values for the next iteration. (If a duplicate value were in the window we would already have returned true, because |x - x| = 0 <= valueDiff, so `erase(value)` can never remove a copy that a later element still needs.) `long long` is used so `x - valueDiff` and `x + valueDiff` cannot overflow `int`.

**(a) Complexity: O(n log k).** The loop runs n times. The set never holds more than k = `indexDiff` elements, so each tree operation costs O(log k):
- `lower_bound` (member of `set`): O(log k), as stated in the STL documentation;
- `end()`, the comparisons and the Boolean logic: O(1);
- `insert`: O(log k);
- `erase(key)`: O(log k).
A constant number of O(log k) and O(1) operations in sequence is O(log k); n iterations give **O(n log k)** time, worst case (balanced BST, no hashing involved). Space: O(k).

**(b) Why not `unordered_set`?** A hash container cannot answer "smallest element >= v" (successor/range queries): the elements are not ordered and there is no `lower_bound`. Scanning the whole window for an element in the interval would cost O(k) per step, O(nk) total. Ordered containers (balanced BSTs) support lower_bound in O(log k).

questions.prompt:
**Two Sum (Leetcode Hashing Questions slide).** Given `nums` and `target`, return the indices `{i, j}` (with `i < j`) of two *different* elements whose values add to `target`. Assume exactly one such pair exists. It must be faster than trying all pairs.

```cpp
vector<int> twoSum(const vector<int>& nums, int target) {
    // write code here
}
```

Also give the time and space complexity of your solution. Justify it the way we do in class: name the cost of every container operation you use (say average vs worst case for the hash-based ones), and combine them with the loop structure.

questions.answer:
```cpp
vector<int> twoSum(const vector<int>& nums, int target) {
    unordered_map<int, int> indexOf;                 // value -> index of an EARLIER element with that value
    for (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {
        auto it = indexOf.find(target - nums[i]);    // is the complement already seen?
        if (it != indexOf.end())
            return {it->second, i};
        indexOf[nums[i]] = i;                        // store after the lookup so an element is never paired with itself
    }
    return {};                                       // no pair (problem statement guarantees one exists)
}
```

**Idea:** for each element we ask "have I already seen the value that would complete the pair?" - one hash lookup of `target - nums[i]`. Storing `nums[i]` *after* the lookup guarantees we never pair an element with itself (so `target = 6`, `nums = {3, 2, 4}` correctly gives `{1, 2}`, not `{0, 0}`), and for repeated values (`{3, 3}`, target 6) the earlier copy is already in the map when the second 3 is processed. If a value occurs twice only the later index is stored, which is fine because a pair is found at the moment the second member is processed.

**Complexity.** Loop: n iterations (n = `nums.size()`). Per iteration: `find` O(1) average, subtraction O(1), `operator[]` assignment O(1) average. Total **O(n) average** (O(n^2) worst case if all keys collide); space **O(n)**. Brute force over all pairs is O(n^2); sorting + two pointers is O(n log n) but loses the original indices unless you also store them.

questions.prompt:
**Valid Anagram (Leetcode Hashing Questions slide: "basic hash map to count occurrences").** Return `true` if `t` is a rearrangement of the letters of `s` (same letters with the same multiplicities).

```cpp
bool isAnagram(const string& s, const string& t) {
    // write code here
}
```

Also give the time and space complexity of your solution. Justify it the way we do in class: name the cost of every container operation you use (say average vs worst case for the hash-based ones), and combine them with the loop structure. Then compare with the approach "sort both strings and compare them".

questions.answer:
```cpp
bool isAnagram(const string& s, const string& t) {
    if (s.size() != t.size()) return false;          // different lengths can never be anagrams
    unordered_map<char, int> count;
    for (char c : s) ++count[c];                     // count[c]++ creates 0 then increments
    for (char c : t) {
        auto it = count.find(c);
        if (it == count.end() || it->second == 0)    // t has a letter s doesn't have (or has too many of it)
            return false;
        --it->second;
    }
    return true;                                     // equal lengths + no count went negative => all counts are 0
}
```

**Why the final `return true` is justified:** the sizes are equal, every character of `t` found an unused copy in `s` (no count ever went below 0), and `t` used exactly `|t| = |s|` copies in total - so every count of `s` was used up and all counts are 0.

**Complexity.** Let n = `s.size()` = `t.size()` (we return early if they differ, O(1)). The first loop does n `operator[]` increments, each O(1) average; the second loop does n `find` + decrement, each O(1) average. Total **O(n) average** (O(n^2) worst case for adversarial hashing, though the key type `char` has at most 256 distinct values, so in practice the map has O(1) entries). Space: O(sigma) where sigma is the alphabet size (<= 256, or 26 for lowercase letters), i.e. O(1) for a fixed alphabet - never more than O(n).

**Sorting approach:** `sort(s)` and `sort(t)` are O(n log n) each, then comparing is O(n): **O(n log n)** time, O(1) extra space if sorting copies in place. The counting approach is faster (O(n)) at the price of the count map.

questions.prompt:
**Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters (Leetcode Hashing Questions slide: "best one is a hash map tracking the last position of each character").** Return the length of the longest substring of `s` in which no character occurs twice. Example: `"abcabcbb"` -> 3 (`"abc"`), `"pwwkew"` -> 3 (`"wke"`), `"abba"` -> 2.

```cpp
int lengthOfLongestSubstring(const string& s) {
    // write code here
}
```

(a) Write the one-pass solution that maps each character to the index of its last occurrence. (b) Give its complexity. (c) What is the complexity of the "obvious" solution that, for each start index, extends the substring until a repeat, and why is it worse?

questions.answer:
**(a)**

```cpp
int lengthOfLongestSubstring(const string& s) {
    unordered_map<char, int> last;                   // char -> index of its most recent occurrence
    int best = 0;
    int start = 0;                                   // current window is s[start..i]
    for (int i = 0; i < (int)s.size(); ++i) {
        auto it = last.find(s[i]);
        if (it != last.end() && it->second >= start) // repeat INSIDE the window (occurrences before start don't count)
            start = it->second + 1;                  // jump the left edge just past the previous occurrence
        last[s[i]] = i;
        best = max(best, i - start + 1);
    }
    return best;
}
```

The window is `s[start..i]` and always has all-distinct characters. When `s[i]` was last seen at index p *inside* the window (p >= start), the new window must start at p + 1. The test `it->second >= start` is essential: a character last seen *before* `start` is no longer in the window and must not move `start` backwards. Trace on `"abba"`: i=0 'a' -> start 0, best 1; i=1 'b' -> best 2; i=2 'b' seen at 1 >= 0 -> start = 2, best stays 2; i=3 'a' seen at 0 but 0 < start = 2, so *no* jump (without the `>= start` test, start would go back to 1 and give a wrong length 3).

**(b) Complexity: O(n) average** (n = `s.size()`). One pass, i from 0 to n-1, i.e. n iterations. Each iteration does one `find` (O(1) average), one `operator[]` assignment (O(1) average), and O(1) arithmetic with `max`. The left edge `start` never moves left, but we never loop over it - it only jumps - so there is no inner loop. Total n x O(1) = O(n) average (O(n^2) worst case with a terrible hash, though the key type `char` has at most 256 values). Space: O(min(n, sigma)) = O(1) for a fixed alphabet.

**(c) Brute force.** For each of n start indices i, extend j while `s[j]` is not in a set of the current characters (set operations O(1) average):

```cpp
int lengthOfLongestSubstringBrute(const string& s) {
    int best = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < (int)s.size(); ++i) {
        unordered_set<char> seen;
        for (int j = i; j < (int)s.size() && seen.insert(s[j]).second; ++j)
            best = max(best, j - i + 1);
    }
    return best;
}
```

The inner loop can run up to the length of the run (up to min(n - i, sigma) steps because a repeat is forced after sigma+1 characters), so the cost is O(n x min(n, sigma)): O(n^2) for large alphabets, O(n x sigma) for a fixed alphabet. It re-examines characters it has already seen; the last-position map lets the left edge jump directly past the previous occurrence, so every character is processed once.

questions.prompt:
**Intersection of Two Linked Lists (Leetcode Hashing Questions slide: "just hash table but slightly more complicated").** Two singly linked lists may share a common tail (after the first shared node, they are the *same* nodes in memory). Return a pointer to the first shared node, or `nullptr` if they do not intersect. Do not modify the lists.

```cpp
struct ListNode {
    int val;
    ListNode *next;
    ListNode(int x) : val(x), next(nullptr) {}
};

ListNode *getIntersectionNode(ListNode *headA, ListNode *headB) {
    // write code here
}
```

(a) Solve it with a hash-based container. (b) Analyze time and space. (c) Why would storing node *values* instead of node *addresses* be wrong? (d) Name a solution that uses O(1) extra space and give its complexity.

questions.answer:
**(a)**

```cpp
ListNode *getIntersectionNode(ListNode *headA, ListNode *headB) {
    unordered_set<ListNode*> seen;                   // store node ADDRESSES, not values
    for (ListNode *p = headA; p != nullptr; p = p->next)
        seen.insert(p);
    for (ListNode *p = headB; p != nullptr; p = p->next)
        if (seen.count(p))                           // first node of B that is also in A
            return p;
    return nullptr;
}
```

**(c)** Two different nodes can hold the same `val` without being the same node; intersection means "same node object", i.e. the same address. Hashing values would report a false match (e.g. A = 1->2->3, B = 9->2->7 share a value 2 but no node). `std::hash<T*>` exists, so `unordered_set<ListNode*>` is legal.

**(b) Complexity.** Let m = length of A and n = length of B. The first loop does m iterations of `insert` (O(1) average each): O(m). The second loop does at most n iterations of `count` (O(1) average each) with O(1) pointer advance: O(n). Total **O(m + n) average** (O(m^2 + mn) worst case with all-colliding hashing); extra space **O(m)** for the set (store the shorter list to make it O(min(m, n))).

**(d) O(1)-space version (two pointers):**

```cpp
ListNode *getIntersectionNodeTwoPointers(ListNode *headA, ListNode *headB) {
    ListNode *a = headA, *b = headB;
    while (a != b) {                                 // each pointer walks lenA + lenB nodes in total
        a = (a != nullptr) ? a->next : headB;
        b = (b != nullptr) ? b->next : headA;
    }
    return a;                                        // the intersection node, or nullptr if there is none
}
```

Each pointer walks its own list and then switches to the head of the other, so both walk exactly m + n nodes in total; they therefore reach the first shared node at the same step (the tails after it have equal length), or both become `nullptr` simultaneously if there is none. Time **O(m + n)** worst case (deterministic, no hashing), space **O(1)**.

questions.prompt:
**Kth Largest Element in a Stream (HW6 Part 3).** Design a class `KthLargest`:
- `KthLargest(int k, vector<int>& nums)` initializes the object with the integer `k` and the initial stream `nums`;
- `int add(int val)` appends `val` to the stream and returns the element that is the **kth largest** in the stream so far (kth largest in *sorted* order, so duplicates count separately). Assume the stream always has at least `k` elements when `add` is called.

Hints from the HW: declare the heap and `k` as data members; `priority_queue` is a **max-heap** by default, and a min-heap is declared as `priority_queue<int, vector<int>, greater<int> > myMinHeap;`.

```cpp
class KthLargest {
    // write code here
public:
    KthLargest(int k, vector<int>& nums) {
        // write code here
    }
    int add(int val) {
        // write code here
    }
};
```

Give the complexity of the constructor (n = `nums.size()`) and of `add`, justifying each operation, and explain why the heap stores only k elements and why it is a *min*-heap.

questions.answer:
```cpp
class KthLargest {
    priority_queue<int, vector<int>, greater<int>> minHeap;   // holds the k largest values seen; top() is the kth largest
    int k;
public:
    KthLargest(int k, vector<int>& nums) : k(k) {
        for (int x : nums) add(x);
    }
    int add(int val) {
        if ((int)minHeap.size() < k)
            minHeap.push(val);
        else if (val > minHeap.top()) {              // val beats the current kth largest
            minHeap.pop();
            minHeap.push(val);
        }
        return minHeap.top();
    }
};
```

**Why a min-heap of size k.** We only ever need the k largest values seen so far. If we keep exactly those k in a **min**-heap, the root `top()` is the smallest of the k largest, i.e. the **kth largest** overall, available in O(1). A new value `val` can matter only if it is larger than the current root (otherwise it is not among the k largest, and discarding it cannot change the kth largest). If it is larger, the old root is no longer among the k largest, so we `pop()` it and `push(val)`. A max-heap would put the *largest* on top, which is the wrong end to evict and to report.

**Complexity.** The heap never has more than k elements.
- `push`: O(log k) (percolate up in a heap of at most k elements); `pop`: O(log k) (deleteMin: percolate down); `top`, `size`, comparison: O(1).
- **`add`: O(log k) worst case** (constant number of operations of cost <= O(log k)).
- **Constructor: O(n log k)** - it calls `add` once per initial element (n x O(log k)). Space: **O(k)**.

(Details: `pop()` returns `void`, so read `top()` *before* popping if you need the value. `top()` on an empty heap is undefined, which is why the problem guarantees at least k elements.)

questions.prompt:
**HW6 Part 4.** The `KthLargest` solution built on `std::priority_queue` pushes and pops through the adaptor's interface. How could you make it more efficient if you manipulated the underlying vector directly (using the heap algorithms of `<algorithm>`, or your own percolation) instead of the `priority_queue` methods? Write the improved class and state what exactly improved, asymptotically and in constant factors.

```cpp
class KthLargest {
    vector<int> heap;   // you manage this vector as a 0-indexed min-heap
    int k;
    // write code here
public:
    KthLargest(int k, vector<int>& nums);
    int add(int val);
};
```

questions.answer:
Two improvements: (1) *build* the initial heap in O(n) instead of n pushes; (2) when a large value replaces the root, do **one** percolate-down instead of a `pop()` followed by a `push()`.

```cpp
class KthLargest {
    vector<int> heap;                                // 0-indexed MIN-heap of the k largest values; heap[0] = kth largest
    int k;
    void percolateDown(int hole) {                   // textbook-style: move the hole down instead of swapping
        int n = (int)heap.size();
        int tmp = heap[hole];
        int child;
        for (; 2 * hole + 1 < n; hole = child) {
            child = 2 * hole + 1;
            if (child + 1 < n && heap[child + 1] < heap[child])
                ++child;                             // pick the smaller child
            if (heap[child] < tmp) heap[hole] = heap[child];
            else break;
        }
        heap[hole] = tmp;
    }
public:
    KthLargest(int k, vector<int>& nums) : k(k) {
        vector<int> v(nums);
        if ((int)v.size() > k)                       // quickselect: the k largest end up in v[0..k-1], O(n) average
            nth_element(v.begin(), v.begin() + k, v.end(), greater<int>());
        heap.assign(v.begin(), v.begin() + min((int)v.size(), k));
        make_heap(heap.begin(), heap.end(), greater<int>());   // buildHeap: O(k)
    }
    int add(int val) {
        if ((int)heap.size() < k) {
            heap.push_back(val);
            push_heap(heap.begin(), heap.end(), greater<int>());
        } else if (val > heap[0]) {
            heap[0] = val;                           // replace the root directly ...
            percolateDown(0);                        // ... and fix with ONE percolate-down (no pop + push)
        }
        return heap[0];
    }
};
```

**What changed.**
1. **Constructor O(n) average instead of O(n log k).** `nth_element(..., greater<int>())` (quickselect) moves the k largest values to the front in O(n) average; `make_heap` then builds the k-element heap in O(k) (the buildHeap bound: the sum of heights is O(k), not k log k). The version with the adaptor does n calls to `add`, each O(log k). (If the initial `nums` has <= k elements we just `make_heap` them in O(n).)
2. **`add` with a replacement: one percolate-down instead of pop + push.** With `priority_queue`, replacing the root costs `pop()` (percolate the last element down) *and* `push()` (percolate the new element up): two O(log k) operations plus the `top()` check. Overwriting `heap[0]` and percolating down once is a single O(log k) walk (<= log2 k levels, 2 comparisons per level, and the textbook "hole" trick uses one assignment per level instead of a swap). Asymptotically `add` is still **O(log k)**; the saving is a smaller constant, plus no extra push when the value is rejected.
3. Direct access `heap[0]` is the kth largest (same as `top()`; no change in cost).

Note the standard-library limitations behind the question: `priority_queue` hides its container (the protected member `c`), offers only push/pop/top, and has no "replace top" / `decreaseKey`; with `<algorithm>` you could emulate replace-top by `pop_heap` + overwrite `back()` + `push_heap`, but that is the same two-pass cost, which is why the answer percolates by hand.

