    // write code here
}
```

Give the complexity in terms of n = number of words and L = length of the longest word.

questions.answer:
```cpp
vector<vector<string>> groupAnagrams(const vector<string>& words) {
    unordered_map<string, vector<string>> groups;    // sorted letters -> all words with those letters
    for (const string& w : words) {
        string key = w;
        sort(key.begin(), key.end());                // O(L log L)
        groups[key].push_back(w);                    // hash key O(L); [] creates an empty vector the first time
    }
    vector<vector<string>> result;
    result.reserve(groups.size());
    for (auto& kv : groups)
        result.push_back(move(kv.second));           // O(1) per group (moves the vector)
    return result;
}
```

**Idea.** Two words are anagrams exactly when their letters sorted are equal, so the sorted word is a canonical key: `groups["aet"]` collects `eat`, `tea`, `ate`. `groups[key]` creates an empty vector the first time the key is seen (the same `operator[]` behavior as with counts), so the `push_back` works immediately.

**Complexity.**
- For each of the n words: copying and sorting the key is O(L log L) (comparison sort of at most L characters); hashing the key string is O(L); `operator[]` lookup is O(1) average **bucket accesses** but each key comparison costs up to O(L), so O(L) per lookup in total; `push_back(w)` is amortized O(1) vector steps plus an O(L) string copy.
- Per word: O(L log L) + O(L) + O(L) = O(L log L). For n words: **O(n L log L) average** (the hash worst case adds a factor: O(n) collisions each costing O(L)).
- The final loop visits each group once and moves its vector in O(1): O(g) <= O(n). Space: **O(n L)** for the keys and the stored words.

(If the alphabet is small and fixed you can build the key by counting letters, O(L) per word and O(n L) total, instead of sorting.)

questions.prompt:
**K most frequent elements.** Given `nums` and `k` (1 <= k <= number of distinct values), return the `k` values that occur most often, **most frequent first** (ties may be broken arbitrarily). Required: time better than sorting all the distinct values, i.e. O(n log k), using a hash map and a heap of size `k`.

```cpp
vector<int> topKFrequent(const vector<int>& nums, int k) {
    // write code here
}
```

Give the complexity in terms of n = `nums.size()`, m = number of distinct values, and k. Why does the heap have to be a **min**-heap even though we want the *most* frequent values?

questions.answer:
```cpp
vector<int> topKFrequent(const vector<int>& nums, int k) {
    unordered_map<int, int> count;
    for (int x : nums) ++count[x];                           // O(n) average
    typedef pair<int, int> P;                                // (count, value)
    priority_queue<P, vector<P>, greater<P>> heap;           // MIN-heap on count: top = least frequent of the k kept
    for (const auto& kv : count) {                           // m = number of distinct values
        heap.push(P(kv.second, kv.first));                   // O(log k)
        if ((int)heap.size() > k) heap.pop();                // O(log k): evict the least frequent
    }
    vector<int> result;
    while (!heap.empty()) {
        result.push_back(heap.top().second);
        heap.pop();
    }
    reverse(result.begin(), result.end());                   // most frequent first
    return result;
}
```

**Why a min-heap.** The heap holds the k best candidates so far; each new candidate has to be compared with the *weakest* of them. In a min-heap on the count, `top()` is the least frequent of the k kept, so after pushing a (k+1)th candidate we `pop()` the minimum, which is exactly the one we want to discard. A max-heap would put the *best* element on top and we could not evict the worst cheaply. Pairs compare by `first` (the count) first, which is what we want; `greater<P>` turns the default max-heap into a min-heap.

**Complexity.**
- Counting: n x `operator[]` O(1) average = **O(n)**.
- Heap phase: iterate over the m entries of the map (O(m), iterating a hash is linear in its size); for each, `push` is O(log k) and, when the size exceeds k, `pop` is O(log k) (the heap never holds more than k + 1 elements). **O(m log k)**.
- Output: k x (`top` O(1) + `pop` O(log k) + `push_back` amortized O(1)) = O(k log k); `reverse` is O(k).
- Total **O(n + m log k + k log k) = O(n + m log k)** (since k <= m); and since m <= n this is at most **O(n log k)**. Space: O(m) for the map + O(k) for the heap.

**Comparison:** sorting the m (value, count) pairs would be O(n + m log m); bucketing the values by count gives O(n); quickselect on the counts is O(n) average. The heap is the one that works on a stream of counts and uses only O(k) extra memory beyond the map.

