**Merge k sorted lists with a heap.** `lists` contains k singly linked lists, each sorted in ascending order (some may be empty/`nullptr`). Return the head of a single sorted list containing all the nodes (relink the existing nodes; do not allocate new ones). Use a `priority_queue` of `ListNode*`; you must supply the comparator so that the node with the **smallest** `val` is on top.

```cpp
struct ListNode {
    int val;
    ListNode *next;
    ListNode(int x) : val(x), next(nullptr) {}
};

ListNode *mergeKLists(vector<ListNode*>& lists) {
    // write code here
}
```

Let N be the total number of nodes. Give the complexity and compare it with (i) merging the lists into a result one after another and (ii) concatenating everything and calling `sort`.

questions.answer:
```cpp
ListNode *mergeKLists(vector<ListNode*>& lists) {
    auto cmp = [](ListNode *a, ListNode *b) { return a->val > b->val; };   // greater-than => MIN-heap on val
    priority_queue<ListNode*, vector<ListNode*>, decltype(cmp)> heap(cmp);
    for (ListNode *head : lists)
        if (head != nullptr) heap.push(head);                // one candidate per list
    ListNode dummy(0);
    ListNode *tail = &dummy;
    while (!heap.empty()) {
        ListNode *node = heap.top();                         // smallest front node among all lists
        heap.pop();
        tail->next = node;
        tail = node;
        if (node->next != nullptr) heap.push(node->next);    // its list's next node becomes a candidate
    }
    return dummy.next;
}
```

**Idea.** At any time the next node of the output is the smallest of the *current front nodes* of the k lists. The heap holds at most one node per list (at most k nodes), `top()` gives the global minimum, and when we take a node we push its successor from the same list. The comparator `a->val > b->val` is the "less" relation of a **min**-heap: `priority_queue` keeps the element that is *largest according to the comparator* on top, so reversing the comparison puts the smallest `val` on top (the same reason `greater<int>` produces a min-heap). The dummy head node avoids a special case for the first node.

**Complexity.**
- Initial pushes: at most k pushes onto a heap of at most k elements: O(k log k).
- Main loop: every one of the N nodes is pushed once and popped once. The heap has at most k elements, so `push` and `pop` are each O(log k); `top`, `empty`, pointer assignments are O(1). That is N x O(log k).
- Total **O(N log k)** (plus O(k) to scan the vector of heads; the k log k term is dominated when N >= k, which holds when no list is empty). Extra space **O(k)** for the heap (nodes are relinked in place).

**Comparison.** (i) Merging lists one after another into a growing result: merging the i-th list into a result of size up to N costs O(N) each, so **O(N k)** worst case. (ii) Concatenate + `sort`: **O(N log N)**, which ignores the sortedness and is larger than O(N log k) whenever k < N. Divide and conquer (merge lists in pairs, like mergesort) also achieves O(N log k) with O(1) extra space.

questions.prompt:
**Running median.** Design a class that supports `addNum(int)` and `findMedian()` on a growing stream. `findMedian()` returns the median of all numbers added so far (the middle value if the count is odd, the mean of the two middle values if even; it is only called after at least one `addNum`). Use **two priority queues**. Both operations should be as fast as possible.

```cpp
class MedianFinder {
    // write code here
public:
    void addNum(int num) {
        // write code here
    }
    double findMedian() const {
        // write code here
    }
};
```

Give the complexity of each method. Why is keeping a sorted `vector` and inserting each number worse?

questions.answer:
```cpp
class MedianFinder {
    priority_queue<int> lo;                                  // MAX-heap: the smaller half (top = its largest)
    priority_queue<int, vector<int>, greater<int>> hi;       // MIN-heap: the larger half (top = its smallest)
public:
    void addNum(int num) {
        if (lo.empty() || num <= lo.top()) lo.push(num);
        else hi.push(num);
        if (lo.size() > hi.size() + 1) {                     // rebalance: sizes must satisfy |lo| - |hi| in {0, 1}
            hi.push(lo.top());
            lo.pop();
        } else if (hi.size() > lo.size()) {
            lo.push(hi.top());
            hi.pop();
        }
    }
    double findMedian() const {                              // precondition: at least one number added
        if (lo.size() > hi.size()) return lo.top();
        return (lo.top() + (double)hi.top()) / 2.0;
    }
};
```

**Invariant.** `lo` is a max-heap holding the smaller half of the numbers and `hi` is a min-heap holding the larger half, with every element of `lo` <= every element of `hi`, and `lo.size()` equal to `hi.size()` or exactly one larger. Then the median is `lo.top()` (odd count) or the mean of `lo.top()` and `hi.top()` (even count). `addNum` first puts the new number on the correct side, then moves **one** element across if the sizes broke the invariant (an element moved from `lo` to `hi` is the largest of the smaller half, so it is <= everything already in `hi`; symmetrically for `hi` to `lo`).

**Complexity.**
- `addNum`: `top()`/`empty()`/`size()` O(1); at most one `push` into a heap of at most n elements: O(log n); and at most one `top`/`pop`/`push` rebalancing step: O(log n) + O(log n). A constant number of O(log n) operations: **O(log n) per addNum**; n additions cost O(n log n).
- `findMedian`: only `top()` and `size()` calls: **O(1)**.
- Space: O(n).

**Why not a sorted vector?** `lower_bound` finds the position in O(log n) but `vector::insert` in the middle shifts the following elements: O(n) per insertion, O(n^2) for n numbers. A `multiset` plus a median iterator is also O(log n) per add, but needs careful iterator maintenance; the two-heap design is simpler.

questions.prompt:
**Detect duplicates within distance d (sliding-window set).** Write a function that returns `true` iff there exist indices `i < j` with `j - i <= d` and `a[i] == a[j]`. Unlike the "last position" map solution, use only an `unordered_set<int>` that stores *values* (no indices): keep exactly the last `d` values in it.

```cpp
bool hasDuplicateWithin(const vector<int>& a, int d) {
    // write code here
}
```

State the time and space complexity. What is the space advantage over the map solution, and when is it a real advantage?

questions.answer:
```cpp
bool hasDuplicateWithin(const vector<int>& a, int d) {
    unordered_set<int> window;                       // before iteration i: the values at indices i-d .. i-1
    for (int i = 0; i < (int)a.size(); ++i) {
        if (window.count(a[i])) return true;
        window.insert(a[i]);
        if (i >= d)
            window.erase(a[i - d]);                  // a[i-d] is now too far from index i+1
    }
    return false;
}
```

**Window invariant.** Before iteration i the set contains `a[i-d .. i-1]` (at most d values). If `a[i]` is in the set, an equal value occurred at distance <= d: return `true`. Otherwise we insert `a[i]`, and once i >= d we erase `a[i-d]`, because that position is more than d away from the next index i + 1. Since we return as soon as the window would contain a duplicate, the window never contains two equal values, so `erase(value)` never deletes a copy that is still needed. For d = 0 the window is always empty and the answer is `false` (correct: distance 0 would need i = j).

**Complexity.** The loop runs n times (n = `a.size()`); each iteration does a `count` (O(1) average), an `insert` (O(1) average) and an `erase(key)` (O(1) average): **O(n) average** (O(nd) worst case if every operation degenerates to O(size) = O(d)). Space: **O(min(n, d))** because the set never holds more than d elements.

**Advantage over the map solution:** the map version stores every distinct value of the input, O(n) space; the window set stores only the last d, so when d << n (e.g. d = 3 on millions of values) memory is much smaller. If d >= n the two use the same O(n).

questions.prompt:
**Count pairs with a given difference.** Count the pairs of positions `i < j` with `|a[i] - a[j]| == k`, where `k >= 0`. Values may repeat (each position counts separately), so e.g. `a = {1, 5, 3, 4, 2}`, `k = 2` gives 3 pairs (1,3), (5,3), (4,2). The count can exceed `int`, return `long long`. It should run in O(n) average time.

```cpp
