    // write code here
}
```

Be careful with `k == 0`. Explain why.

questions.answer:
```cpp
long long countPairsWithDiff(const vector<int>& a, int k) {
    unordered_map<long long, int> count;             // value -> how many EARLIER elements have it
    long long pairs = 0;
    for (int x : a) {
        auto it = count.find((long long)x - k);      // earlier element equal to x - k
        if (it != count.end()) pairs += it->second;
        if (k != 0) {                                // for k == 0, x - k and x + k are the same value: count it once
            it = count.find((long long)x + k);       // earlier element equal to x + k
            if (it != count.end()) pairs += it->second;
        }
        ++count[x];                                  // insert x only AFTER looking, so i < j is respected
    }
    return pairs;
}
```

**Idea.** Process the elements left to right and keep `count[v]` = number of earlier elements equal to `v`. For the current x, the earlier partners are the elements equal to `x - k` and to `x + k`; add both counts. Inserting x *after* looking enforces `i < j`, so each unordered pair is counted exactly once (when its later member is processed).

**k == 0.** Then `x - k` and `x + k` are the same value, and adding both would count every pair twice; the `if (k != 0)` guard counts it once. (For `k > 0` the two values are different, so no pair is double counted.) `long long` keys and the cast `(long long)x - k` avoid overflow for values near `INT_MAX`/`INT_MIN`.

**Complexity.** n iterations; per iteration at most two `find` calls (O(1) average each), O(1) additions, and one `operator[]` increment (O(1) average): **O(n) average**, O(n^2) worst case with a degenerate hash; space O(n). The brute force examines all n(n-1)/2 pairs, O(n^2); sorting and sweeping with two pointers is O(n log n).

