    // write code here
}
```

(a) Solve it with an `unordered_set`. (b) Give the complexity. (c) Give a solution with a *better worst-case bound* and say why it is possible here.

questions.answer:
**(a)**

```cpp
vector<int> findMissing(const vector<int>& nums) {
    int n = (int)nums.size();
    unordered_set<int> present(nums.begin(), nums.end());    // range constructor: n inserts, O(n) average
    vector<int> missing;
    for (int v = 1; v <= n; ++v)
        if (present.count(v) == 0)
            missing.push_back(v);                            // appended in increasing order
    return missing;
}
```

**(b) Complexity.** The range constructor does n inserts, each O(1) average: O(n) average. The loop runs n times, each `count` is O(1) average and each `push_back` amortized O(1): O(n). Total **O(n) average** (O(n^2) worst case with a degenerate hash); space O(n).

**(c) Better worst case: direct addressing.** The values are known to lie in `[1, n]`, so a hash function is unnecessary - the value itself is a perfect index:

```cpp
vector<int> findMissingNoHash(const vector<int>& nums) {
    int n = (int)nums.size();
    vector<bool> present(n + 1, false);              // direct addressing: value v lives at index v
    for (int x : nums) present[x] = true;
    vector<int> missing;
    for (int v = 1; v <= n; ++v)
        if (!present[v]) missing.push_back(v);
    return missing;
}
```

Initializing the `vector<bool>` is O(n), the first loop is n x O(1), the second loop is n x O(1) with amortized O(1) `push_back`: **O(n) in the worst case** (deterministic, no collisions possible), space O(n) bits. This is the same idea as a hash table with `hash(x) = x` and no collisions; it is only possible because the key universe is small and dense.

