```

Hint: maintain a map value -> count of occurrences inside the window. What must you do when a count reaches zero, and why?

questions.answer:
```cpp
vector<int> distinctInWindows(const vector<int>& a, int k) {   // assumes k >= 1
    vector<int> result;
    unordered_map<int, int> count;                   // value -> occurrences inside the current window
    for (int i = 0; i < (int)a.size(); ++i) {
        ++count[a[i]];                               // a[i] enters the window
        if (i >= k) {                                // a[i-k] leaves the window
            auto it = count.find(a[i - k]);
            if (--it->second == 0)
                count.erase(it);                     // erase by iterator: O(1), no second lookup
        }
        if (i >= k - 1)
            result.push_back((int)count.size());     // number of distinct values = number of keys
    }
    return result;
}
```

**Idea.** The window slides one step at a time: one element enters, one leaves. A map `value -> occurrences inside the window` is updated in O(1) average per step, and the number of distinct values is the number of keys, `count.size()` (O(1)). When a value's count drops to 0 we must **erase** the key; otherwise `size()` would still count it as present. Order inside the loop matters little as long as the window is `a[i-k+1 .. i]` when we read the size: add `a[i]`, remove `a[i-k]`, and start reporting from i = k - 1 (the first full window). Example check: windows of `{1,2,1,3,4,2,3}`: `{1,2,1,3}` -> 3, `{2,1,3,4}` -> 4, `{1,3,4,2}` -> 4, `{3,4,2,3}` -> 3.

**Complexity.** n iterations. Per iteration: one `operator[]` increment (O(1) average), one `find` for the departing element (O(1) average), a decrement and an `erase(iterator)` (O(1)), `size()` (O(1)) and an amortized O(1) `push_back`. **O(n) average** (O(nk) worst case if every map operation costs O(size) = O(k)). Space **O(k)**: the map never holds more than k keys. Recounting each window from scratch would cost O(k) per window, O(nk) in total.

questions.prompt:
**Longest consecutive run.** Given an unsorted vector `nums`, return the length of the longest set of values that are consecutive integers (e.g. `{100, 4, 200, 1, 3, 2}` -> 4, from 1, 2, 3, 4). The order in `nums` does not matter and duplicates are possible. Required: **O(n) average time** - sorting (O(n log n)) is not allowed.

```cpp
int longestConsecutive(const vector<int>& nums) {
    // write code here
}
```

Argue carefully why your nested loops are O(n) in total and not O(n^2).

questions.answer:
```cpp
int longestConsecutive(const vector<int>& nums) {
    unordered_set<int> s(nums.begin(), nums.end());          // O(n) average; also removes duplicates
    int best = 0;
    for (int x : s) {
        if (s.count(x - 1)) continue;                        // x - 1 exists, so x is NOT the start of a run
        int y = x;
        while (s.count(y + 1)) ++y;                          // walk the run starting at x
        best = max(best, y - x + 1);
    }
    return best;
}
```

**Idea.** Put all values into a hash set. A value `x` is the **start of a run** exactly when `x - 1` is not in the set. Only for starts do we walk upward with `count(y + 1)` until the run ends. Values that are not starts are skipped in O(1).

**Complexity (why the nested loops are O(n), not O(n^2)).**
- Building the set: n inserts x O(1) average = O(n).
- The outer loop visits each of the d <= n distinct values once; each does one `count(x - 1)`: O(d) = O(n).
- The inner `while` only starts at run starts. Each run is walked exactly once, from its start to its end, and different runs are disjoint, so across the whole algorithm every distinct value is touched by the inner loop **at most once** (as a member of its own run). The total number of inner steps is therefore at most d, not "number of starts x run length" in the naive sense of n x n. Each inner step is one `count`, O(1) average.
- Total: O(n) + O(n) + O(n) = **O(n) average** (O(n^2) worst case with a degenerate hash), space O(n).

Without the `if (s.count(x - 1)) continue;` test, every element of a run would walk the rest of the run, which is O(n^2) for one long run.

questions.prompt:
**The selection problem with heaps (Textbook 6.4 / HW6 context).** Return the `k` smallest elements of `a` in ascending order (`0 <= k`; if `k > a.size()` return all of them). Write **two** versions:

(A) build a heap of **all** n elements in O(n) and call `pop` k times;
(B) scan `a` once, keeping a heap that never holds more than `k` elements.

```cpp
vector<int> kSmallestBuildHeap(const vector<int>& a, int k) {
    // write code here
}
vector<int> kSmallestHeapK(const vector<int>& a, int k) {
    // write code here
}
```

Give the complexity of each in terms of n and k. Which is better when k is tiny? When k is close to n? Which one works on a stream / needs only O(k) memory?

questions.answer:
**(A) buildHeap + k deleteMins.**

```cpp
vector<int> kSmallestBuildHeap(const vector<int>& a, int k) {      // textbook 6A: buildHeap + k deleteMins
    priority_queue<int, vector<int>, greater<int>> heap(a.begin(), a.end());   // range constructor = make_heap: O(n)
    vector<int> out;
    for (int i = 0; i < k && !heap.empty(); ++i) {
        out.push_back(heap.top());
        heap.pop();                                          // O(log n) each
    }
    return out;
}
```

Constructing a `priority_queue` from an iterator range copies the n elements into the vector and calls `make_heap`, which is the textbook buildHeap: **O(n)**. Each `pop()` is a deleteMin, O(log n), and `top()` is O(1): k x O(log n). Total **O(n + k log n)**, space O(n).

**(B) heap of size k.**

```cpp
vector<int> kSmallestHeapK(const vector<int>& a, int k) {          // textbook 6B: heap of size k
    priority_queue<int> heap;                                // MAX-heap holding the k smallest seen so far
    for (int x : a) {
        if ((int)heap.size() < k)
            heap.push(x);                                    // O(log k)
        else if (!heap.empty() && x < heap.top()) {          // x beats the largest of the k kept
            heap.pop();                                      // O(log k)
            heap.push(x);                                    // O(log k)
        }
    }
    vector<int> out;
    while (!heap.empty()) {
        out.push_back(heap.top());
        heap.pop();
    }
    reverse(out.begin(), out.end());                         // ascending
    return out;
}
```

The heap is a **max**-heap holding the k smallest seen so far, so its `top()` is the largest of them - the one to evict when a smaller element arrives (a min-heap would be the wrong way round). Each of the n elements costs O(1) for the comparison with `top()` and, if it enters, a `pop` plus a `push`, each O(log k): **O(n log k)** worst case. Reading the heap out costs O(k log k) (k pops), which is dominated by n log k. Space **O(k)**; it never needs to see the elements twice, so it works on a stream.

**Comparison.** (A) is O(n + k log n), (B) is O(n log k). For k = O(n / log n) (A) is linear: e.g. k = sqrt(n) gives O(n) for (A) but O(n log n) for (B). For constant k both are O(n). For k = Theta(n) both are O(n log n). The decisive difference is memory and streaming: (A) must store all n elements (O(n) space) and see them all before the first answer, (B) needs only O(k) space and works on a stream - which is why the "kth largest in a stream" problem uses (B). Sorting everything is O(n log n); `nth_element` (quickselect) is O(n) on average.

questions.prompt:
**Subarrays with a given sum.** Count the contiguous, non-empty subarrays of `nums` whose elements add up to `k`. Values may be negative, so a sliding window does not work. Example: `{1, 2, 3}`, `k = 3` -> 2 (`{1,2}` and `{3}`); `{1, -1, 0}`, `k = 0` -> 3. Required: O(n) average.

```cpp
