    // write code here
}
```

questions.answer:
```cpp
int subarraySum(const vector<int>& nums, int k) {
    unordered_map<long long, int> seen;              // prefix sum value -> number of prefixes with that sum
    seen[0] = 1;                                     // the empty prefix
    long long sum = 0;
    int count = 0;
    for (int x : nums) {
        sum += x;                                    // sum = nums[0] + ... + nums[i]
        auto it = seen.find(sum - k);                // a subarray ending at i sums to k iff some earlier prefix = sum - k
        if (it != seen.end()) count += it->second;
        ++seen[sum];
    }
    return count;
}
```

**Idea.** Let `P[i]` be the sum of the first i elements. The subarray `nums[a..b]` sums to `P[b+1] - P[a]`. It equals `k` exactly when `P[a] = P[b+1] - k`. While scanning, `sum` is the current prefix `P[b+1]`; `seen[v]` counts how many earlier prefixes (including the empty prefix, `P[0] = 0`) have value `v`, so `seen[sum - k]` is the number of subarrays ending at the current element with sum `k`. The initial `seen[0] = 1` accounts for subarrays that start at index 0. We look up *before* inserting the current prefix so a subarray is never empty. Negative numbers are fine because nothing assumes the prefix sums are monotone. `long long` avoids overflow of the running sum.

**Complexity.** n iterations; each does one `find` (O(1) average), an addition, and one `operator[]` increment (O(1) average): **O(n) average**, O(n^2) worst case; space **O(n)**. Brute force over all start/end pairs with a running sum is O(n^2).

