Give the complexity in terms of m = `a.size()` and n = `b.size()`. How would the complexity change with `std::set` instead?

questions.answer:
```cpp
vector<int> intersectDistinct(const vector<int>& a, const vector<int>& b) {
    unordered_set<int> inA(a.begin(), a.end());      // O(|a|) average
    vector<int> result;
    for (int x : b)
        if (inA.erase(x) > 0)                        // erase(key) returns how many were removed (0 or 1) => each value reported once
            result.push_back(x);
    return result;
}
```

Putting `a` in a set removes its duplicates. For each element of `b` we call `erase(x)`, which removes the key if present and returns the number of elements removed (0 or 1): a hit both tests membership and guarantees that a repeated value in `b` is not reported a second time (it is gone from the set).

**Complexity.** Building the set: m inserts x O(1) average = O(m). Loop: n iterations x (`erase(key)` O(1) average + amortized O(1) `push_back`) = O(n). Total **O(m + n) average** (O(mn) worst case if all hash values collide), space O(m) (choose the smaller input for the set to get O(min(m, n))).

**With `std::set`** the same code is correct but every insert/erase costs O(log m) worst case: building is O(m log m), the loop O(n log m): **O(m log m + n log m) = O((m + n) log m)**, deterministic. (Sorting both and merging is O(m log m + n log n).)

questions.prompt:
**Range search in an ordered container (Leetcode Hashing Questions slide).** Given a `set<int>` and two bounds `lo`, `hi`, return all elements `x` with `lo <= x <= hi` in increasing order, using `lower_bound` and `upper_bound` and a simple loop from one iterator to the other.

```cpp
vector<int> inRange(const set<int>& s, int lo, int hi) {
    // write code here
}
```

(a) Complexity in terms of n = `s.size()` and m = number of elements returned. (b) What goes wrong if `lo > hi` and you have no guard? (c) Why can't an `unordered_set` do this efficiently?

questions.answer:
```cpp
vector<int> inRange(const set<int>& s, int lo, int hi) {
    vector<int> out;
    if (lo > hi) return out;                         // without this guard the loop below can run past end()
    auto first = s.lower_bound(lo);                  // first element >= lo   : O(log n)
    auto last = s.upper_bound(hi);                   // first element >  hi   : O(log n)
    for (auto it = first; it != last; ++it)          // m steps, each O(1) amortized
        out.push_back(*it);
    return out;
}
```

`lower_bound(lo)` returns an iterator to the first element **not less than** `lo` (i.e. >= lo); `upper_bound(hi)` returns an iterator to the first element **greater than** `hi` (i.e. > hi). So the half-open iterator range `[first, last)` contains exactly the elements in the closed interval `[lo, hi]`.

**(a) Complexity: O(log n + m).** `lower_bound` and `upper_bound` are member functions of `set`, each a tree search: O(log n). The loop makes m steps; `++it` on a set iterator is O(1) amortized (a full in-order traversal of m elements takes O(m) steps in total), and `push_back` is amortized O(1). Total O(log n) + O(log n) + O(m) = **O(log n + m)**. Do not use `std::lower_bound(s.begin(), s.end(), lo)` here: for non-random-access iterators it must advance one node at a time, which costs O(n).

**(b) `lo > hi`.** Then `first` can be *after* `last` in the sorted order (e.g. `lo = 50`, `hi = 10`: `first` points at the first element >= 50, `last` at the first element > 10), so the loop `it != last` never meets `last`, walks past `end()` and has undefined behavior. The guard `if (lo > hi) return {}` avoids it.

**(c) Hash container.** An `unordered_set` has no order, so no `lower_bound`/`upper_bound` and no range iteration; you would have to scan all n elements: O(n) per query. Ordered containers (balanced BSTs) support range queries; hashing only supports exact-match lookup.

