**LRU cache with `list` + `unordered_map`.** Design `LRUCache` with a fixed `capacity >= 1`:
- `int get(int key)`: return the value for `key`, or `-1` if absent; a hit makes the key the *most recently used*.
- `void put(int key, int value)`: insert or update `key`; the key becomes most recently used. If inserting a new key would exceed the capacity, first evict the *least recently used* key.

Both operations must run in **O(1) average** time. Use a `std::list` and an `std::unordered_map`.

```cpp
class LRUCache {
    // write code here
public:
    LRUCache(int capacity);
    int get(int key);
    void put(int key, int value);
};
```

Explain the role of each container, why neither alone is enough, and why the iterators stored in the map stay valid.

questions.answer:
```cpp
class LRUCache {
    int cap;
    list<pair<int, int>> items;                                  // (key, value); front = most recently used
    unordered_map<int, list<pair<int, int>>::iterator> where;    // key -> its node in items
public:
    LRUCache(int capacity) : cap(capacity) {}                    // assumes capacity >= 1
    int get(int key) {
        auto it = where.find(key);
        if (it == where.end()) return -1;
        items.splice(items.begin(), items, it->second);          // move that node to the front: O(1), iterators stay valid
        return it->second->second;
    }
    void put(int key, int value) {
        auto it = where.find(key);
        if (it != where.end()) {                                 // key exists: update and refresh
            it->second->second = value;
            items.splice(items.begin(), items, it->second);
            return;
        }
        if ((int)items.size() == cap) {                          // full: evict the least recently used (back)
            where.erase(items.back().first);
            items.pop_back();
        }
        items.push_front(make_pair(key, value));
        where[key] = items.begin();
    }
};
```

**Roles.**
- The **list** keeps the keys ordered by recency: front = most recently used, back = least recently used. A doubly linked list can move or remove a node, given an iterator to it, in O(1) (no shifting like a vector).
- The **unordered_map** maps each key to the *iterator* of its node in the list, so we can find the node in O(1) average instead of scanning the list in O(n).
- A list alone makes `get` O(n) (search); a map alone cannot tell which key is least recently used (no usable order, iterating a hash is not in order of use).

**Why the iterators stay valid.** Inserting into or erasing from a `std::list` invalidates only iterators to the erased element; all other iterators and references stay valid. `splice` moves an existing node into a new position without copying or reallocating it, and iterators to the moved element remain valid (they now refer to the same element at its new place). A `vector` would not offer this (insertion/erase shifts elements and can reallocate, invalidating iterators).

**Complexity.**
- `get`: `find` O(1) average; `splice` of one node O(1); reading the value O(1). **O(1) average.**
- `put`: `find` O(1) average; update + `splice` O(1); or `size()` O(1) (C++11), `back()`/`pop_back()` O(1), `erase(key)` from the map O(1) average, `push_front` O(1), `operator[]` insert O(1) average. **O(1) average** (the rare rehash is amortized O(1)); worst case O(n) per operation only with degenerate hashing. Space: O(capacity).

questions.prompt:
**Find and fix the STL bugs.** Each of the six fragments below has one bug (a compile error, undefined behavior, a wrong result, or a hidden performance problem). For each: say what is wrong, say *why* (cite the documented behavior), and write the corrected code.

```text
// (a) Intended: report whether "pear" is in stock WITHOUT changing the map.
unordered_map<string,int> stock;
stock["apple"] = 3;
if (stock["pear"] > 0) cout << "have pear\n";
cout << stock.size() << endl;          // prints 2, expected 1

// (b) Intended: remove and use the smallest element.
priority_queue<int, vector<int>, greater<int> > pq;
pq.push(5); pq.push(2);
int smallest = pq.pop();

// (c) Intended: a MIN-heap of ints.
priority_queue<int> minHeap;
minHeap.push(5); minHeap.push(2);
cout << minHeap.top();                 // prints 5, expected 2

// (d) Intended: delete every key whose count is 1.
unordered_map<int,int> m;
for (auto it = m.begin(); it != m.end(); ++it)
    if (it->second == 1) m.erase(it);

// (e) Intended: count visits per grid cell.
unordered_map<pair<int,int>, int> visits;
visits[make_pair(1, 2)]++;

// (f) Intended: first element >= x of a std::set<int> s, in O(log n).
auto it = std::lower_bound(s.begin(), s.end(), x);
```

questions.answer:
**(a) `operator[]` used for lookup.** `operator[]` returns a reference to the mapped value and **creates** a new element with a default value (0) when the key is absent (cplusplus.com `unordered_map::operator[]`; HW5 hint). So `stock["pear"]` inserted `pear -> 0`, and the size is 2. Use `find` (or `count`):

```cpp
bool inStock(const unordered_map<string,int>& stock, const string& item) {
    auto it = stock.find(item);                 // never inserts; O(1) average
    return it != stock.end() && it->second > 0;
}
```
(A `const` map cannot even use `[]`, which is a useful compile-time reminder.)

**(b) `pop()` returns `void`.** `priority_queue::pop` removes the top but returns nothing (so it can be exception safe); `top()` returns a const reference. Read first, then pop:

```cpp
int takeSmallest(priority_queue<int, vector<int>, greater<int> >& pq) {
    int smallest = pq.top();    // O(1)
    pq.pop();                   // O(log n)
    return smallest;
}
```

**(c) Default priority_queue is a MAX-heap** (`less<int>` compares, so the "largest" is on top). A min-heap needs the three-argument form with `greater`:

```cpp
priority_queue<int, vector<int>, greater<int> > minHeap;   // top() is the smallest
```

**(d) Using an erased iterator.** `m.erase(it)` invalidates `it` (only iterators to erased elements are invalidated), and then `++it` is undefined behavior. `erase(iterator)` returns the iterator to the next element; advance only in the else branch:

```cpp
void removeSingles(unordered_map<int,int>& m) {
    for (auto it = m.begin(); it != m.end(); ) {
        if (it->second == 1) it = m.erase(it);   // C++11: returns the next iterator
        else ++it;
    }
}
```
(Related trap: **inserting** into an `unordered_map` while iterating can trigger a rehash, which invalidates *all* iterators, though not references or pointers to elements. Never insert during iteration; collect keys first.)

**(e) No `std::hash` for `pair`.** `unordered_map<pair<int,int>,int>` does not compile because the standard provides no hash for `pair`. Either supply a hash functor, encode the pair into one integer key, or use an ordered `map<pair<int,int>,int>` (pair has `operator<`, O(log n) per access):

```cpp
struct PairHash {
    size_t operator()(const pair<int,int>& p) const {
        return hash<long long>()(((long long)p.first << 32) ^ (unsigned int)p.second);
    }
};
unordered_map<pair<int,int>, int, PairHash> visits;
```

**(f) `std::lower_bound` on a `set`.** It compiles, and does only O(log n) *comparisons*, but a set's iterators are bidirectional, so the algorithm has to advance them one step at a time: **O(n)** total (cplusplus.com: on non-random-access iterators the iterator advance is linear). Use the member function, which walks the tree in O(log n):

```cpp
int firstAtLeast(const set<int>& s, int x) {   // assumes such an element exists
    return *s.lower_bound(x);                  // member of std::set: O(log n)
}
```

