    // write code here
}
```

Let n = `words.size()`, m = number of distinct words, and L = the maximum word length. Give the complexity. Explain precisely which element the comparator must put on top of the heap and why.

questions.answer:
```cpp
vector<string> topKFrequentWords(const vector<string>& words, int k) {
    unordered_map<string, int> count;
    for (const string& w : words) ++count[w];
    typedef pair<string, int> P;
    // comp(a, b) == true means "a has HIGHER priority to stay than b" (a is better): more frequent, or equal and alphabetically smaller.
    // priority_queue's top() is the element that is "largest" under comp, i.e. the WORST kept word: exactly the one to evict.
    auto better = [](const P& a, const P& b) {
        if (a.second != b.second) return a.second > b.second;
        return a.first < b.first;
    };
    priority_queue<P, vector<P>, decltype(better)> heap(better);
    for (const auto& kv : count) {
        heap.push(P(kv.first, kv.second));
        if ((int)heap.size() > k) heap.pop();                    // drops the worst of the k+1
    }
    vector<string> result(heap.size());
    for (int i = (int)result.size() - 1; i >= 0; --i) {          // pops come out worst-first: fill from the back
        result[i] = heap.top().first;
        heap.pop();
    }
    return result;
}
```

**The comparator.** `priority_queue<T, Container, Compare>` keeps on top the element that is *greatest according to `Compare`* (`Compare(a, b)` returning `true` means "a comes before b / a is smaller in priority"; with the default `less<T>` the biggest element ends on top). To evict the weakest word in O(log k) we need the **worst** word on top. Define `better(a, b)` = "a is a better answer than b": higher count, or equal count and the alphabetically smaller word. Passing `better` as `Compare` makes the *best* the "smallest" and the **worst** word (lowest count, and among equals the alphabetically larger) the "greatest", i.e. the top. So after each `push`, `pop` removes the worst of the k + 1 candidates. When the loop ends the heap holds the k best, and popping them returns worst first, so we fill the result from the back. (This is the same reason `greater<int>` makes a min-heap: the comparator's notion of "less" is what the heap treats as lower priority.)

**Complexity.**
- Counting: n words, each `operator[]` hashes a string of length <= L: O(L) average, so **O(nL)** average.
- Heap phase: m pushes and at most m pops on a heap of size <= k + 1: m x O(log k) heap steps, each step compares two pairs, and comparing two strings is O(L): **O(m L log k)**.
- Output: k pops x O(L log k) = O(k L log k) <= O(m L log k).
- Total **O(nL + m L log k)** average (hash worst case adds more). Space O(mL) for the map + O(kL) for the heap. If strings are treated as constant size (as in the HW5 sequences), this is O(n + m log k).

questions.prompt:
**Sorting a nearly sorted array.** Every element of `a` is at most `k` positions away from the position it will have in the fully sorted array (a "k-sorted" array; `k = 0` means already sorted). Sort `a` in ascending order in **O(n log k)** time using a heap that holds at most `k + 1` elements. Write the result back into `a`.

```cpp
void sortKSorted(vector<int>& a, int k) {
    // write code here
}
```

Justify correctness (why the smallest of the heap is always the next sorted element) and give the complexity. Compare with `std::sort`.

questions.answer:
```cpp
void sortKSorted(vector<int>& a, int k) {
    priority_queue<int, vector<int>, greater<int>> heap;     // MIN-heap holding at most k+1 elements
    size_t out = 0;                                          // next position of a to overwrite
    for (size_t i = 0; i < a.size(); ++i) {
        heap.push(a[i]);                                     // a[i] has been read, so a[out] (out <= i) is free
        if (heap.size() > (size_t)k) {
            a[out++] = heap.top();                           // smallest of the window is the next sorted element
            heap.pop();
        }
    }
    while (!heap.empty()) {                                  // flush the last <= k elements
        a[out++] = heap.top();
        heap.pop();
    }
}
```

**Why it is correct.** The element that belongs at sorted position p is at most k positions from p in `a`, i.e. it lies among `a[0 .. p + k]`. When we have read `a[0 .. p + k]` and removed the p smallest elements (positions 0 .. p-1), the heap holds `k + 1` elements of the unprocessed window, and the smallest of them is the (p)th smallest overall: any element after index p + k is at least as large as the true p-th smallest because it belongs at position >= p + 1. So `top()` is the next sorted element. After the scan ends, the heap contains the last <= k elements, which we flush in order. Writing is safe in place: when we write `a[out]` we have already read `a[0 .. i]` and `out <= i`, so no unread element is overwritten.

**Complexity.** The heap never holds more than k + 1 elements, so each `push` and `pop` is O(log k). Each of the n elements is pushed once and popped once: n x (O(log k) + O(log k)) = **O(n log k)** time (the final flush is included in those n pops), extra space **O(k)**. For `k = 0` it is O(n) (pushes and pops of a 1-element heap). `std::sort` ignores the structure and costs O(n log n); the heap version is asymptotically better whenever k = o(n), e.g. O(n) for constant k, and it can also process the data as a stream with O(k) memory.

