```cpp
class MinHeap {
    vector<int> v;                                   // the heap lives in a plain vector
public:
    bool empty() const { return v.empty(); }
    size_t size() const { return v.size(); }
    int top() const { return v.front(); }            // O(1): the root is v[0]
    void push(int x) {                               // O(log n)
        v.push_back(x);                              // amortized O(1)
        push_heap(v.begin(), v.end(), greater<int>());   // percolate the new last element up
    }
    void pop() {                                     // O(log n)
        pop_heap(v.begin(), v.end(), greater<int>());    // moves the root to v.back(), re-heapifies the rest
        v.pop_back();                                // O(1): discard the old root
    }
    explicit MinHeap(const vector<int>& init) : v(init) {
        make_heap(v.begin(), v.end(), greater<int>());   // buildHeap: O(n)
    }
    MinHeap() {}
};
```

**(a)** The heap functions maintain the invariant "no element is *less than* its children" under the comparator you give; by default `operator<` (a max-heap). A heap built with `greater<int>()` is ordered for `>`; calling `push_heap` or `pop_heap` with the default comparator afterward would look for a max-heap, treat the min-heap as broken, and corrupt it (undefined results: the functions require the range to be a heap *with respect to the same comparator*). The comparator must therefore be identical in `make_heap`, `push_heap`, `pop_heap` (and `sort_heap`, `is_heap`).

**(b)** `pop_heap(first, last, comp)` swaps the root `*first` with the last element `*(last-1)` and then restores the heap property on the shortened range `[first, last-1)` by percolating the moved element down. It does **not** shrink the container: the old root is now sitting at `v.back()`, outside the heap range, and `v.pop_back()` is what actually removes it (O(1)). Likewise `push_heap(first, last)` expects the new element to be already in the last slot (`push_back` first) and percolates it up.

**(c) Complexities.** `top`: `v.front()`, **O(1)**. `push`: amortized O(1) for `push_back` + O(log n) for `push_heap` (percolate up) = **O(log n)**. `pop`: `pop_heap` O(log n) (percolate down; cplusplus.com: up to about 2 log n comparisons) + `pop_back` O(1) = **O(log n)**. Constructor from a vector: copy O(n) + `make_heap` O(n) = **O(n)** (buildHeap), instead of O(n log n) for n pushes. `empty`, `size`: O(1).

